WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.840
Test 2. This is the IELTS listening test. You will hear a number of different recordings

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:14.720
and you will have to answer questions on what you hear. There will be time for you to read

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:21.240
the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to check your work. All the

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:29.639
recordings will be played once only. The test is in four parts. At the end of the test you

00:00:29.639 --> 00:00:36.439
will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet.

00:00:36.439 --> 00:00:47.919
Now turn to part 1. Part 1. You will hear a woman who is caring for

00:00:47.919 --> 00:00:55.720
her elderly mother talking to a friend. First you have some time to look at questions 1

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:20.720
to 4. Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 4.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:29.239
It's really good to see you Tom. Since I had to give up work I feel I'm losing touch

00:01:29.239 --> 00:01:34.519
with my friends and colleagues. We really miss you in the office. We were all so sorry

00:01:34.519 --> 00:01:39.480
you had to leave. But you must be relieved to have more time to look after your mother.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:45.519
How is she? Well she's very cheerful but she needs a lot of help. Have you tried to get

00:01:45.519 --> 00:01:51.680
any support from the local council? No, I didn't know I could. Yes, they offer different

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.680
kinds of practical support. They realise that carers sometimes need time for all the other

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.559
responsibilities they have apart from the person they're caring for and also that they

00:02:01.559 --> 00:02:10.160
sometimes need a break. Absolutely. Okay, so tell me more. How do I go about getting

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.479
this support? Well you'd have to have an assessment of your mother's needs. That means someone

00:02:15.479 --> 00:02:20.240
would come round and talk to you about the situation and what you need. So for a start

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.399
they'd want to know the amount of time you spend looking after your mother every day.

00:02:24.399 --> 00:02:28.960
Okay. Then they'll probably ask you what sorts of tasks you do for your mother during the

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:34.800
day. Things like if she needs help with getting dressed for example. Right, I help her with

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.720
that and also I help her get into the shower in the morning. Yes, that sort of thing. They'll

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.800
probably ask you if you do the shopping for her and help her at mealtimes and whether

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:52.160
she can cope using money. Yes, that's becoming a bit of a problem. She used to be very good

00:02:52.160 --> 00:03:01.639
at it but not anymore. Before you hear the rest of the conversation you have some time

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:04.399
to look at questions 5 to 10.

00:03:31.639 --> 00:03:53.600
So recently I've noticed she's started to have quite bad problems with her memory. If

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:59.880
I wasn't there I think she'd forget to eat for example. And often she doesn't seem quite

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:05.720
sure what day it is. Yes, tell them about that. And are there any physical difficulties

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:13.800
you have caring for her? Lifting her for example. Yes, she's quite heavy and I'm afraid of hurting

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:20.200
my back. I'd be in real trouble if that happened. They can give you advice about that and also

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:26.200
about how to avoid the possibility of your mum having a fall. Great. So once they've

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:32.760
done this assessment, if I'm eligible, what happens next? Well, they might support you

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:38.720
financially. So they might help you with transport costs, like if you have to get a taxi to take

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.760
your mother for an appointment for example. I usually drive her myself actually. So could

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:51.000
I claim for the petrol? You could. And you can claim for the insurance too. Oh, right.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.839
And if you need help with the housework they can arrange for someone to come along once

00:04:54.839 --> 00:05:00.600
or twice a week. And one other thing, I hope you don't mind me saying this, but it's important

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:06.519
you look after yourself and it seems to me you're under quite a bit of stress. I am, yes. Well,

00:05:06.519 --> 00:05:12.600
tell the council because they may be able to give you some advice on how to minimise it. Really?

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:20.279
Though actually I feel so much better having talked to you. I'll get in touch with the

00:05:20.279 --> 00:05:24.519
council straight away. Now, shall we go for coffee or something?

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:38.519
That is the end of part one. You now have one minute to check your answers to part one.

00:05:50.279 --> 00:05:53.239
You now have one minute to check your answers to part one.

